# Building the Environment

Turning the design into code meant an environment that trains on plain integers, and an agent wrapper around it that speaks in the actual items;

the same kind of boundary the project had already drawn elsewhere between internal reasoning and external interface.

That wrapper was also built to match the existing agents' code structure exactly, so a trained policy could eventually sit in the same benchmark as designed agents.

A few decisions carried straight over from the audit:

the secret is redrawn on every episode, not reused, and a solved episode is now distinguished from a timed out one at the environment level, the same distinction the corrected benchmark makes.

One idea didn't survive contact with implementation.

Automatically growing the puzzle size during training, ran into a real limitation:

a policy network's input and output shapes are fixed at construction, and changing the puzzle size mid training changes both.

That's real design work on its own, so it was shelved rather than shipped half working.

---

## The Observation That Kept Changing

The one piece that went through several honest attempts was the feature meant to summarize the guess history;

which items are still plausible where.

The first version only reacted to a guess that scored exactly zero, which is correct but weak;

most guesses score above zero, and those contributed nothing at all.

The next attempt borrowed directly from the Hybrid Agent's candidate-pool logic:

sample permutations, bias them toward the current best guess, keep the ones consistent with history, and read off a real distribution.

It worked, but it also brought back exactly the kind of complexity the project had already spent a full cycle moving away from on the heuristic side;

a sampling budget, and a hit rate to worry about.

A simpler, fully deterministic version was tried next.

The zero score restriction was kept, with one additional check per cell:

swap the item into that position on the current best guess and test that single result against the full history.

No sampling. Nothing to tune.

Tested directly, it collapsed entirely;

every cell was ruled out, including the secret's actual placements.

The reason was the same one already learned once, in a different shape:

demanding that one arbitrarily modified guess satisfy every accumulated constraint at once is an extremely strict bar,

and there was no reason it should ever pass.

> One failed candidate proves that candidate doesn't work. It doesn't prove nothing does.

The honest choice was to drop that second check rather than patch it further,

and accept the simpler, weaker signal as a known trade off instead of a partially fixed one.

---

## Where This Leaves Things

Nothing has actually been tested yet.

The real test is still ahead:

* Train the policy;

* run it through the same benchmark as the four heuristic agents;

* and see whether a learned swap strategy can actually beat what Logic and Hybrid already do by hand.
